window.docContents[191].resolve({"/Preface/#Type-Systems--Preface--Overview":{"contents":"To reason about a program, we represent it as a mathematical object\ntogether with a mathematical function or relation for describing its\nbehavior. Our main approach to doing so is called operational semantics,\nand it amounts to defining a kind of abstract interpreter for\nthe programs in a language. We present two styles of operational semantics:\nthe big-step style specifies a program's behavior by relating it directly to its final\nvalue, while the small-step style specifies a program's behavior as\na series of computational steps. The small-step approach is more involved,\nbut also makes it possible to reason about a program's evaluation in a more\nfine-grained manner.\n\nAfter introducing operational semantics using a simple language of arithmetic\nand boolean expressions (Slang), we turn to types, the volume's\ncentral subject. A type is a static classification of what a program computes,\nand a type system is a static reasoning system that uses types to rule out\nwhole categories of run-time errors before a program is ever run. Working with a\nsmall language of numbers and booleans, we define a type system for it and\nprove the two signature theorems of type-system metatheory — progress (a\nwell-typed term is either a value or can take a step) and preservation\n(taking a step preserves well-typedness) — which together guarantee that a\nwell-typed program can never \"go wrong.\"\n\nFinally, we turn our attention to the simply typed lambda-calculus (STLC),\na much richer language with a more interesting type system. We start from a\nsmall untyped core and extend it with numbers, pairs, sums, lists, and\ngeneral recursion, proving progress and preservation anew for the extended\nlanguage. We then study subtyping, a mechanism that lets a term of one\ntype stand in for a term of another — motivated by the way record types\nbehave in object-oriented languages — and prove progress and preservation\nonce more for a language with subtyping. In so doing, we work our way up\nto a fairly full featured programming language.\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009Preface","header":"1.2. Overview","id":"/Preface/#Type-Systems--Preface--Overview"},"/Smallstep/#Type-Systems--Smallstep___-Small-step-Operational-Semantics--Multi-Step-Reduction":{"contents":"\n\ninductive Multi {α : Type} (R : Relation α) : α → α → Prop where\n  | refl (x : α) : Multi R x x\n  | step (x y z : α) (h₁ : R x y) (h₂ : Multi R y z) : Multi R x z\n\n\n\n\nWe write ⟶* for the Multi Step relation on terms\n\nnotation:40 t:41 \" ⟶* \" t':41 => Multi Step t t'\n\n\nThe relation Multi R has several crucial properties.\n\n\n\nattribute [refl] Multi.refl\n\nexample : (.c 5 : Tm) ⟶* .c 5 := by rfl\n\n\n\n\nexample : (.p (.c 1) (.c 2)) ⟶* .c (1 + 2) := by\n  apply Multi.step (y := .c (1 + 2))\n  · exact .plus 1 2\n  · rfl\n\n\n\n\ntheorem multi_single {α : Type} (R : Relation α) (x y : α)\n    (h : R x y) : Multi R x y := by\n  apply Multi.step (y := y)\n  · exact h\n  · rfl\n\n\n\n\ntheorem multi_trans {α : Type} (R : Relation α) (x y z : α)\n    (g : Multi R x y) (h : Multi R y z) : Multi R x z := by\n  induction g with\n  | refl a => exact h\n  | step a b c h₁ h₂ ih =>\n      apply Multi.step (y := b)\n      · exact h₁\n      · exact ih h\n\n\n\n\nWhich of the following relations on numbers cannot be expressed as\nMulti R for some R?(A) less than or equal\n(B) strictly less than\n(C) equal\n(D) none of the above\n\n(B) strictly less than\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009Smallstep: Small-step Operational Semantics","header":"3.4. Multi-Step Reduction","id":"/Smallstep/#Type-Systems--Smallstep___-Small-step-Operational-Semantics--Multi-Step-Reduction"}});